WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.664
Batch size tuning is bounded by hardware.

2
00:00:02.664 --> 00:00:11.265
GPU VRAM is usually the first constraint people notice because model weights, runtime buffers, and the KV cache all compete for space.

3
00:00:11.265 --> 00:00:21.693
System RAM matters too, especially when the model is partially offloaded, when the runtime uses CPU memory for staging, or when the machine falls back to swapping.

4
00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:27.858
Once swapping begins, performance can collapse even if the process does not crash immediately.

5
00:00:27.858 --> 00:00:33.034
Memory bandwidth and compute utilization are just as important as raw capacity.

6
00:00:33.034 --> 00:00:38.590
A GPU with enough VRAM may still be underused if the runtime cannot feed it efficiently.

7
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:44.527
A CPU-only setup may be limited by memory bandwidth, thread scheduling, and cache behavior.

8
00:00:44.527 --> 00:00:52.443
Integrated GPUs share memory with the system, so aggressive settings can affect both inference and the rest of the machine.

9
00:00:52.443 --> 00:00:59.978
Context length has a direct impact because longer conversations and larger documents require more attention state.

10
00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:07.894
The KV cache stores intermediate state that lets generation continue without reprocessing the entire context every time.

11
00:01:07.894 --> 00:01:10.406
That cache is useful, but it is not free.

12
00:01:10.406 --> 00:01:18.169
More concurrent sequences, longer contexts, and larger batch-related settings can increase memory pressure quickly.

13
00:01:18.169 --> 00:01:30.500
Oversized batch settings can look attractive because they promise more parallelism, but they can also cause out-of-memory errors, runtime instability, high latency, or slower throughput.

14
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:37.350
The practical goal is to find a setting that keeps the hardware busy without pushing it into a fragile state.

15
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:44.505
Stable headroom matters, especially for systems that need to run for hours instead of surviving one benchmark.

16
00:01:44.505 --> 00:01:49.148
This is why a stable configuration usually leaves deliberate headroom.

17
00:01:49.148 --> 00:01:58.967
A machine that reports just enough free VRAM during a clean test may fail when the prompt is longer, another process opens, or concurrent requests arrive.

18
00:01:58.967 --> 00:02:01.098
Headroom is not wasted capacity.

19
00:02:01.098 --> 00:02:08.329
It is the margin that keeps inference predictable when real operating conditions are less tidy than a benchmark.
